  {"id":"3de5168e-c3d4-4f24-8461-63dc660b4aef","term":"Absolute automation","verdict":"MINOR","suggestions":[{"field":"practical_application","severity":"Medium","issue":"The definition says absolute writes 'replace any existing automation for that parameter over the duration of the pass,' and practical_application lumps Write, Touch, and Latch together as doing this. But the common_mistakes entry singles out only Write mode for 'erasing good automation on the next playback pass.' A beginner (this term is tagged beginner) is left with a contradiction: if all three modes replace over the whole pass, why is only Write dangerous? This can make them fear Touch/Latch or misuse Write.","suggestion":"Clarify the distinction: Write replaces existing automation continuously across the whole pass (hence the erase risk), whereas Touch and Latch also write absolute values but only where/while the control is actively engaged, leaving untouched sections intact. E.g., 'Write overwrites the entire pass; Touch and Latch write absolute values only while you are moving the control, so they overwrite less.'"}]},
